FTX  Celsius block Phi a wave of bankruptcies  is hitting the crypto industry and  policy makers are calling for regulation  regulatory framework we really need to  make sure that that environment has the  appropriate regulatory guard rails to  find out how Washington might regulate  the crypto industry moving forward let's  look at a few past SEC investigations  that reveal how the agency sees crypto  in 2020 the SEC sued cryptocurrency  company Ripple over the company's coin  xrp it is by far the biggest piece of  litigation that the SEC has against a  cryptocurrency the agency charged two  Rebel Executives with engaging in an  illegal Securities offering by selling  xrp the agency argued that it wasn't a  currency it was a security making the  distinction using the Securities Act of  1933. the law says that if something is  an investment contract or one form of a  security it can be regulated under this  act but the courts didn't clarify what  that was until a Supreme Court case in  1946. to call something an investment  contract it has to pass a test aptly  named the Howie test there's just three  questions  first has someone invested money then is  there a common Enterprise is the success  of the buyer intertwined with the seller  and third is the buyer led to expect  profits derived from others if all the  answers are yes it's a security the  Ripple lawsuit says you know the SEC are  holding the ground and insisting that  this whole new world of crypto is really  just a new form of the same old function  which is to issue a kind of an asset in  order to raise money to build a business  but some crypto companies like Ripple  don't accept this idea they say their  products aren't Securities they're  Commodities like gold they say that  digital assets with a real world purpose  don't trade because of the seller there  is no common Enterprise so the tokens  aren't Securities Ripple said the SEC  was acting outside their legal limits  and they would continue to fight the  case in September both parties filed  motions asking a federal judge to render  a verdict with this case the SEC took  its biggest shot at broadly establishing  its jurisdiction over the crypto market  then a year later the SEC took the same  law and applied it to a new part of the  industry crypto lending it started  investigating block thigh a platform  that allowed users to loan crypto to  others for trading blockfy was doing  something that the SEC had not yet  enforced against and the SEC said that  that transaction that offering that  blocked that I was doing was also an  investment contract which is a form of a  security for the case the agency's  reasoning was this the way blockwise  product worked is that it borrowed  crypto assets from customers with the  promise to repay it with interest that  fast easy said counted as investment of  money blockfi would lend those assets to  institutional borrowers and keep some  profits for itself there's common  Enterprise and based on block 5  statements about paying interest the  company LED investors to expect profit  so blockvis crypto lending accounts  passed the Howie test making it in the  sec's eyes a security the SEC moving  into trying to regulate a crypto lending  products  shows you that a lot of this crypto  ecosystem resembles traditional finance  and the SEC therefore is going to look  at that and say  that looks like the kind of thing we  regulate blockfy settled for 100 million  dollars the highest fine ever agreed to  by a cryptocurrency company block 5 CEO  Zach Prince said the company cooperated  with Regulators we have a long history  of compliance and working with  regulators and this is another example  of that block fi which filed for  bankruptcy in November did not reply to  a request for comment but the case  expanded the sec's use of the Howie test  to regulate crypto showing the agency  was willing to Target non-traditional  digital assets then later in 2022 the  agency took aim at a different part of  the industry celebrity endorsements  though don't mean that an investment  product is right for you or even frankly  that it's legitimate  the SEC was Prosecuting celebrities for  advertising crypto  for example let's look at the agency's  recent action against Kim Kardashian  in September 2021 Kardashian posted a  video and swipe up story on Instagram  promoting ethereum Max tokens and was  paid 250 000 for the Post  the SEC said that because ethereum Max  was a security with you guessed it an  investment of money common Enterprise  and a reasonable expectation of profits  the agency argued that Kardashians post  should be regulated under another part  of the Securities act that said she  couldn't advertise a security without  revealing that ethereum Max paid her the  SEC fined her over a million dollars for  the lack of disclosure Kardashian did  not respond to a request for comment in  these cases because they they involve  celebrities they allow the SEC to  amplify their message to a much broader  audience  than the sec's enforcement actions  typically reach the cases against Ripple  blockfi and Kardashian show the sec's  strategy for applying Securities  regulations to crypto the SEC is going  to continue to apply  these laws to crypto unless and until  Congress decides that crypto should fall  under its own sort of customized  regulatory regime the SEC said in May it  would add 20 new investigators to its  crypto unit but if regulations changed  some of the crypto Market could fall  under the commodity Futures Trading  commission the sec's sister agency we  are going to continue to see a lot of  the same kinds of enforcement actions  but we don't know yet if the strategy is  going to change somehow because of what  happened with FTX but it's likely the  agency will continue on the path of  enforcement in the future  foreign

